    {
      q: "What time zone does India use?",
      a: "India uses a single national time zone known as Indian Standard Time (IST). It is calculated at an offset of UTC+5:30 and is observed year-round across the entire country."
    },
    {
      q: "What is IST?",
      a: "IST stands for Indian Standard Time. It is the official standard time zone observed throughout the Republic of India, calculated relative to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)."
    },
    {
      q: "Why does India have only one time zone?",
      a: "India uses a single time zone for administrative, economic, and safety reasons. Implementing one unified clock prevents scheduling collisions on the extensive railway network, simplifies national banking, and streamlines federal government operations, despite the physical 2-hour solar time difference between the eastern and western borders."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is IST UTC+5:30 instead of a whole hour?",
      a: "IST is set at UTC+5:30 because it is calculated based on the 82.5° E longitude line, which passes through Mirzapur in Uttar Pradesh. This meridian was chosen during the colonial era because it lies exactly halfway between the 75° E (UTC+5) and 90° E (UTC+6) meridians, splitting the difference for the nation."
    },
    {
      q: "Does India observe Daylight Saving Time?",
      a: "No. India does not observe Daylight Saving Time (DST). Because of its tropical location near the equator, daylight hours remain relatively constant year-round, making seasonal clock changes unnecessary."
