    },
    {
      q: "Does India share its timezone offset with any other country?",
      a: "Yes. Sri Lanka also observes India's timezone offset of UTC+5:30 year-round, which is referred to as Sri Lanka Standard Time (SLST)."
    },
    {
      q: "How does the 30-minute offset affect international business scheduling?",
      a: "The 30-minute offset means meetings scheduled on the hour in the US or UK land on the half-hour in India. For example, a 9:00 a.m. meeting in London (BST, UTC+1) starts at 1:30 p.m. in India, requiring schedulers to carefully check the half-hour alignment."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the difference between IST and GMT?",
      a: "GMT (Greenwich Mean Time) is a solar-based timezone offset at UTC+0. IST (Indian Standard Time) is 5.5 hours ahead of GMT. For example, when it is 12:00 PM GMT in London, it is 5:30 PM IST in India."
    },
    {
      q: "How does domestic travel in India handle time differences?",
      a: "Because India uses a single national time zone, domestic travelers do not need to adjust their watches when flying or traveling by train between states. However, they will notice that sunrise and sunset occur much earlier in the eastern states (like Assam) compared to the western states (like Gujarat)."
    },
    {
      q: "What are the trading hours of the Indian stock markets?",
      a: "The National Stock Exchange (NSE) and Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) in India operate from 9:15 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. IST, Monday through Friday."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is the abbreviation IST sometimes confusing?",
