    <h2>1. What Is India's Time Zone?</h2>
    <p>
      India is home to over 1.4 billion people and occupies a major geographical footprint in South Asia. 
      The country stretches from the mountains of Jammu and Kashmir in the north to the tropical tip of Kanyakumari in the south, and from the deserts of Gujarat in the west to the forests of Arunachal Pradesh in the east. 
      To manage schedules, transport, and commerce across this vast area, India observes a single time zone.
    </p>
    
    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Unlike the United States, Canada, or Australia—which are divided into multiple regional time zones—India uses one unified clock for the entire nation. 
      Whether you are in Mumbai, New Delhi, Kolkata, or Bengaluru, the local time remains exactly the same.
    </div>

    <p>
      This single timezone standard simplifies federal administration, national broadcasting, and transportation logistics. 
      However, it also presents geographical challenges, particularly in the eastern states, where the sun rises and sets significantly earlier than in the west. 
      Understanding how this offset functions is essential for anyone doing business with India, scheduling international flights, or managing offshore software development teams.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 1: India Time Zone Map -->
