  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2: WHAT IS INDIAN STANDARD TIME (IST)?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-is-ist" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What Is Indian Standard Time (IST)?">
    <h2>2. What Is Indian Standard Time (IST)?</h2>
    <p>
      Indian Standard Time (IST) is the official time standard observed throughout the Republic of India. 
      The offset for IST is calculated at exactly **UTC+5:30** (5 hours and 30 minutes ahead of Coordinated Universal Time). 
      This makes India one of the major nations in the world to observe a half-hour offset rather than a whole-hour interval.
    </p>

    <p>
      The history of standard time in India is closely linked with the colonial era:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Colonial Presidencies:</strong> Prior to the late 19th century, major cities observed their own local mean times. 
        The three presidency towns established distinct standards: Bombay Time (UTC+4:51), Calcutta Time (UTC+5:53), and Madras Time (which lay in between).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Railway Standard:</strong> As railway lines expanded across the country, local times created safety hazards. 
        In the late 19th century, railway networks adopted Madras Time (UTC+5:21) as a compromise railroad standard, which was maintained by the Madras Observatory.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Introduction of IST:</strong> Indian Standard Time was officially established on January 1, 1906, using the 82.5° E meridian as the central reference. 
        Despite its introduction, Bombay and Calcutta maintained their local times for decades. 
        Bombay Time was kept until 1948, shortly after India achieved independence, when IST became the sole national standard.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Visual Asset 2: IST UTC+5:30 Infographic -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/ist-utc-530-infographic.webp" 
        alt="IST (UTC+5:30) Infographic showing clock offset from UTC" 
