      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The 30-Minute Shift Challenge:</strong> Because India uses a half-hour offset, meetings scheduled on the hour in the US or UK land on the half-hour in India. 
        For example, a meeting scheduled at 9:00 a.m. in London (BST, UTC+1) starts at 1:30 p.m. IST in India. 
        This requires international schedulers to double-check meeting invites to prevent confusion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>BPO &amp; Outsource Shifts:</strong> To support US clients, many Indian technology firms operate on night shifts (e.g., 6:30 p.m. to 3:30 a.m. IST) to align with standard East Coast business hours (9:00 a.m. to 6:00 p.m. EDT).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stock Market Coordination:</strong> The National Stock Exchange (NSE) and Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) in Mumbai operate from 9:15 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. IST. 
        International institutional investors must align their trading algorithms with these hours.
      </li>
    </ul>

