      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No Watch Adjustments:</strong> Because India uses a single national time zone, domestic travelers flying between Mumbai (west) and Kolkata (east) do not need to adjust their clocks upon arrival.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Early Sunrise of the East:</strong> If you are traveling to northeastern states (like Assam or Arunachal Pradesh), be prepared for the sun to rise as early as 4:15 a.m. in the summer. 
        Local commercial activities and farm work often begin much earlier than in western cities like Mumbai, where sunrise occurs around 6:00 a.m.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Jet Lag Management:</strong> International travelers arriving from the US or Europe must adjust to both a large time shift and a half-hour offset. 
        Gradually shifting sleep schedules by 30-minute steps prior to departure can help reduce jet lag.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

