  <section id="programming-tech" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines">
    <h2>10. Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines</h2>
    <p>
      In software development, managing Indian Standard Time requires strict adherence to international standards to prevent scheduling bugs and database corruption.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 8: India Programming & UTC Workflow -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/india-programming-utc-workflow.webp" 
        alt="Programming workflow showing UTC database storage and client local rendering" 
        title="India Programming & UTC Workflow" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 8: Developer Timezone Database Workflow.</strong> Diagram demonstrating the best practice of storing timezone-agnostic Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) in databases and translating to `Asia/Kolkata` at the client UI layer.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>1. Database Storage</h3>
    <p>
      Always store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) using the ISO 8601 standard formatting: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. 
      Do not store local timestamps directly, as it makes queries across different global client regions highly problematic.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Use IANA Identifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Do not hardcode offsets (like +5.5 or +5:30) in your codebase. 
      Offsets can change due to legislative decisions. Instead, use the official IANA Time Zone Database name for India: **`Asia/Kolkata`** (formerly registered as `Asia/Calcutta`).
    </p>

    <h3>3. JavaScript Localization Example</h3>
    <p>
      To display a database UTC timestamp in India's local standard time, utilize the native `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`const utcDate = new Date('2026-07-15T12:00:00Z');
const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-IN', {
  timeZone: 'Asia/Kolkata',
  dateStyle: 'medium',
  timeStyle: 'long'
});
console.log(formatter.format(utcDate)); 
// Output will render in India local time (IST)`}</pre>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 11: COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid">
    <h2>11. Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Working or scheduling meetings with India involves navigating its unique timezone rules. 
      Avoid these common pitfalls:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing IST with Irish Standard Time:</strong> The abbreviation 'IST' is also used for Irish Standard Time (UTC+1 in summer) and Israel Standard Time (UTC+2 / UTC+3). 
        Always verify the country or specify the UTC offset (UTC+5:30) in your communications to prevent scheduling errors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring the 30-Minute Offset:</strong> Many schedulers forget that India uses a half-hour offset. 
        Assuming that all offsets are in whole hours will result in meetings being scheduled 30 minutes early or late.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming Seasonal Clock Changes:</strong> India does not observe Daylight Saving Time. 
        Do not send calendar invites that automatically adjust India participants' clocks in March or November.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Incorrect Offset Formatting in Code:</strong> When configuring server offsets, ensure you use the proper fractional format (e.g., `+05:30` or `5.5`), rather than assuming integers.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

